Title: Tan Brick House
Credit: Written by
Author: Justin Del Vecchio
Draft date: June 2026

INT. COFFEE SHOP - MORNING

MIKA BOYKO, early 60s, with a Ukrainian accent, waits in line.

The customer ahead of him stares at his phone.

The CASHIER extends a handful of change and waits for the customer.

MIKA
Ahem.

CUSTOMER
Oh hey, thanks! Thanks!

The CUSTOMER collects his change and moves along.

Mika places two dollars, one quarter, and two pennies on the counter.

MIKA
Hi, usual.

MIKA takes coffee, finds table and sits with multiple friends.  

Older, clearly retirees.

FRIEND 1
Hey Mika, how are you doing?

MIKA
OK. Got up early, watched the sunrise, and walked here. Traffic is ridiculous today. Crazy. People speed like mad right by the school.

FRIEND 2
Hey, how's that house you gotta sell?

MIKA
I knew you would bring it up. Knew it. It has been three months now and nothing. Nothing.

Friend 1 smiles.

FRIEND 1
Seems longer than that!

Mika rubs his face.

MIKA
It is not. It is just three months.

Friend 3, at a table across the room, peers over the paper he's reading.

FRIEND 3
What house? What's that?

FRIEND 2
Oh, come on, man. Do you even listen to what we talk about? The house. The house!

FRIEND 3
I've been in Florida for three months!

Mika holds up both hands as if to say everything is OK.

MIKA
I will explain. The house. What is the house? OK. The house is my wife's parents'. Wonderful people. You hear stories about mothers-in-law and the troubles they manufacture. She would do anything for you. No trouble at all. Quiet. Beautiful.

FRIEND 2
I'm really going to have to listen to this all over again. I need another cup.

Friend 2 stands and walks away.

Mika sips his coffee.

MIKA
She would do anything for us.  Her husband, he died back in the 90s.  Smoked like you would not believe.  He was nice, too, mind you.  Just the smoking.  The mother, she died in February.

FRIEND 3 
Sorry about that.

MIKA
Yeah, yeah.  Thanks.  So ... we get the house.  Nona has one sister. Stella. She lives in Austin and she tells Nona 'You sell the house.  Keep the money.  I lived in Austin for last 22 years - you took care of momma all that time.  You should have it.'  The sister, she got some money from the mother and did not, mind you, did not have to clean out all the shit from house.  That was us for four weeks.

FRIEND 2
You got a house my friend!  Easy money.

Friend 3 says in passing while going back to reading the paper.

FRIEND 3
Austin ...

MIKA
Yeah, easy money.  And Austin is OK place.  Visited my sister-in-law there four or five times.  Visited twice in July.  Crazy. Hot, hot, hot.  You cannot leave the house in daytime, could only leave at night.  Reminded me of Egypt where people in the summer only go outside at night.

Mika pauses for more coffee.

MIKA
The coffee is weak today.  Who made this!  OK, so, that is all good.  All fine.  Sister OK with everything.  Wife OK with everything.  All we need to do is sell the house, get the money, move on.

FLASHBACK:

INT. KITCHEN - MORNING

An aging Tudor home's kitchen.

MIKA sits at the table with his wife, NONA, beautiful and impeccably dressed.

She eats a Sausage McMuffin.

MIKA
All we have to do is sell the house.  Put it on the market.  Set a reasonable price, I know what that price is, and we are good.

Nona says somewhat irritated.

NONA
I know.  I know! 

Nona then contemplates while holding the McMuffin.

NONA
We can sell the house and then, we can think about a condo in Ft. Lauderdale.

MIKA
I keep telling you - there is no way we can get anything good in Ft. Lauderdale for the price we can get for this house. No way.  It's not like this house is equivalent to an ocean front in Ft. Lauderdale.

Nona finishes the McMuffin.

NONA
You keep saying that.  But you are not looking hard enough. I see these shows.  I see the Beachfront Bargains, they find these things all the time.  I will find something, let me look.

MIKA
You can look all you want.  All you want.  I will not stop you from doing that.  Download the app, search Ft. Lauderdale.  There is nothing to be had for $200 thousand that gets what I know you want.  Not even close.  It's just ... it's not even realistic Nona!

NONA
Exactly, because you do not let me look.  You are not looking right.

Mika shakes head, exhales, and looks away.

MIKA
Let's just sell this thing first.  Please.  Let's do that.  The house is empty now.  Can we finally sell it.  Then we can argue about Ft. Lauderdale.

NONA
Yes, let's talk about the house.  I know exactly what we need to do.

MIKA
Good, good.  Consensus.  I know too.

NONA
Really, you know what to do.  You have read my mind and know.  Really.

MIKA
Yes, I have.  I have looked at the prices. Houses, comparables.

Nona shaking head

NONA
Comparables.  

MIKA
Yes.  Yes.  We should put a good price on the house.  Go 240 K or 245 K to start and get offers.  Move it out.

NONA
Really.  That is what we should do.

MIKA
Yes.  Really.

Nona smiles confidently.

NONA
This is what we are going to do.

Mika senses what is about to happen.

MIKA
No ... no.

NONA
We are going to use Mikey Derogai.

MIKA
No, no. Why. No. Why? Why Mikey?

NONA
Because, he is a real estate agent.  He knows how to sell a house.  And he knows how to get the best price. And we know his family.

Mika's English is good but at times, grammatically incorrect when agitated.  There is certainly a Slavic accent though not that strong.

MIKA
Oh great!  Great.  So you are providing precise reasons we should not go with Mikey.  What if things go wrong?

NONA
What do you mean, wrong? 

MIKA
Mikey is not able to sell the house, he listens to you and prices it way too high, and he get exactly no one interested in looking at the house.  I know this kid, we know this kid, we know exactly what he is capable of.

NONA
He sold Mira's house.  Two weeks.  Two weeks and the house was sold.  We will have no problems.  None.

MIKA
Oh come on.  Mira's house was perfect.  Perfect.  Updated kitchen, huge yard, you could fit three cars in that garage.  I could have sold that house.  Easy to do.  Your parent's house, this house, not easy.

Nona smiles confidently again after a sip of coffee.

NONA
No.  No. This house is perfect.  It is great. There will be no problems. 

MIKA
It is not great.  Not great.  Not not great.  It's good, it's a good house, yes.  But it has problems.

NONA
Like what?

MIKA
Like the fact the kitchen is out of a 1970s Reader's Digest advertisement.  Like the fact there are three bathrooms with pink tiles.  Pink tiles.  Three. Like the fact every square inch of flooring that is not in the bathroom is carpeting from the 1980s.

NONA
You can stop now.  I do not need to listen to this.  This house - 

She pauses to sip coffee

NONA
This house, is perfect.  It will sell.  Besides, I have already called Mikey.

MIKA
How? When? Why did you do this and not ask me?

Nona speaks perfect English with no accent.

NONA
Um, because, I do not need your permission.  It is my house. I make the decisions.

MIKA
You do not need my permission that is not what I said. 

NONA
Sure sounded like it.

MIKA
Not my permission, just my advice.

NONA
There is some difference between the two?

MIKA
Yes, of course.  Of course there is.  I would have told you 'Mikey, I don't think he is a good choice.'  I see realtors online, I think I know which ones are good.  We get that flyer from the Spencer woman, she seems like she knows how to sell a house.

Nona laughs.

NONA
Well we meet Mikey tomorrow.

MIKA
When?

NONA
4 PM at my parent's house.

MIKA 
Opportunity to discuss?

NONA
Nope.  Do you want more coffee.

MIKA
Please ... yes.

NONA
Oh, it is two, by the way.

MIKA
Two what?

NONA
Two bathrooms with pink tile.  The other is lime green.

EXT. TAN BRICK HOUSE - DAYTIME

A plain tan-brick house from the early '70s.

MIKA checks his watch: 4:20. Nearby, NONA admires the front garden.

A black four-door BMW with tinted windows pulls up. Vanity plate: DEROGAI.

MIKEY, 6'2", handsome, and sharply dressed in gray slacks and a trench coat, steps out. He sets his coffee on the roof, removes his sunglasses, grabs an attache case, and strides toward Mika.

MIKEY
(booming, optimistic)
Mika!  Nona!  It is so good to see you!  

Mikey moves in for a hug.

A giddy Nona goes over for an embrace.

NONA
Oh Mikey, we are so glad you can help!  So great.  How are you!  How is everything!  

MIKEY
Oh Nona, it could not be better.  Could not be better!  Look!

Mikey gestures to BMW.

He goes to shake hands with Mika.

MIKA
You are late.  20 minutes.  Late.

MIKEY
Ohhhh, so sorry.  So sorry.  I had to take get the kids from hockey practice.  It went late.  I wanted to call but I only had your home number and figured you would already be here. 

MIKA
And yet you have coffee.

Mikey walks beside Nona.

MIKEY
Yeah, they sell it at the rink.  What did we do here!

NONA
Don't worry about being late.  We have nothing to do.  Look, I cleaned up the front of the house.

Mikey stops to look at entirety of the house.

MIKEY
Oh every little bit helps Nona.  Every little bit.  This, this, this is a great house.  Great house!

MIKA
Yes, good house.  Not great though.  But good.

MIKEY
No, no, do not undersell this house. This is a GREAT house.

Mikey makes a gesture with his hand as though taking a picture.

NONA.
See!  I keep telling him it is great, Mikey, but he will not listen. He will not listen.

MIKEY
Don't worry Nona, we'll win him over.

Both start laughing.

MIKEY
I already know the price for the house.

MIKA
What?  How they hell do you know the price for this house.  You have not step foot in here, in, what 17 years?  Again, how the hell do you know?

MIKEY
Mika.  Mika!  I know real estate!  I know this stuff.  I looked at the pictures on Google Maps.  I know the exact price.  Nona - 

Mikey looks to Nona.

MIKEY
I know the exact price for this beautiful house, I know.

NONA
Oh Mikey, I know you do.  I know you do.  Come on, let's look at.

MIKA
Why bother? He knows the price.  He doesn't need to look at anything.  By the way, uh, what exactly is the price.  The perfect price.

Mikey stands hands at hip.  

Taking snapshot pose with hands once again he looks to Nona. 

MIKEY
The price.  The price is 320.  320 and not a nickle less.

NONA
Yes!

MIKA
Oh come on! 

MIKEY
Absolutely.  No doubt!  The price is 320 and we can close this baby, smooth deal, in two weeks.

MIKA
You haven't even looked inside.  Mikey, I am sorry.  But you are crazy if you think you are gonna get 320 for this house it's - it -

Mikey interrupts.

MIKEY
Nona.  Listen to this guy.  Listen to him!

Mikey slaps Mika on the back a bit too hard.

MIKEY
The house will sell.  For 320.  I know real estate.  I live and breathe this stuff.  This house will sell for 320.  No doubt.

NONA
I knew it.  That will set us up for something special in Ft. Lauderdale.

MIKA
Its not going to sell for that.  Never going to sell.  And even if it did, 320 gets nothing special in Ft. Lauderdale for that amount.  Sorry.  Nothing.

Mika looks to Mikey.

MIKA
Please, set the price lower.  Please at least look at it before you set the price.

MIKEY
Why go low when you can go high and get the price you want.  The price you need!

MIKA
Mikey, house is not worth that amount.  It should be 240 to 245 at most.  At most.  Make your life.  God, make our life easy.  Just start at a reasonable price, sell it fast and get it done.

Mikey simply laughs and shakes head.

MIKEY
Why 240 may I ask.  Why that price?

MIKA
First, the interior is outdated.  All the rooms, all the rooms need renovations.  All, every last one.  Have seen the popcorn ceilings in there? It's of a type never seen before - like Christmas ornaments.

MIKEY
Ahhhh.... but the house has a good layout.  It has a great layout.  You can't beat that.  And a lot of people like that 70s stuff.  Vintage.  It is coming back.

MIKA
Again, you haven't been in this frigging house in 17 years.  Why don't you walk through.  Just imagine you are buyer.  You have this mental calculator that figures out renos costs. 10, 20, 30, very quickly you get to 60, 80 thousand in renos to make house match the other houses in this neighborhood.

MIKEY
Exactly, it's a great neighborhood.

MIKA
Yeah, it is, it is.  Have you looked at the comparables?  Have you seen houses with this square footage?  Look at how good they are on the interior.  This is, a joke -

Mikey laughs.

MIKEY
Comparables.  Nona, you did not tell me that Mika had a side hustle as a real estate agent.  You should have told me!

NONA
Oh Mikey, let's go inside and look.  The coffee maker is still there.  Do you remember?  The Bunn one. I made coffee and have Danish for you.

MIKA
I am not a fan of Danish.

NONA
Mikey is.

Mika turning attention back to Mikey.

He points as he says this.

MIKA
Mikey, I go two blocks that way and find houses with the same amount of space, less traffic, no updates needed, they go for 2-9-9.  Why the hell would this go for 320.

MIKEY
You forget about the intangibles.

MIKA
What?  What?  What intangibles?  What, that is on high traffic street with crappy interior.  Is on Main Street.  Mikey, Main Street.  Every town has a Main street.  It is always one thing; busy.

MIKEY
Exactly, benefit one.  It's on Main Street.

MIKA
So?

MIKEY
Zoned residential. 

Mikey turns to look at Nona and points to house.

MIKEY
And commercial.

MIKA
So!

MIKEY
You just doubled your market my friend.  Doubled it.  It could work for a family or professional or doctor or lawyer - 

MIKA 
Yes, because lawyers are so interested in doing home renovations and not in sending you $300 invoice for a half hour of work spent on sending out letter that the will reading date has has been set.  Yeah, they love renovations.

NONA
Mika, please listen to Mikey.

MIKA
What?

MIKEY
Mika, Mika, lets look inside the house.  I am going to show how we are going to get 3-2-0 for this. 

Group heads up driveway to front door.  

Mikey opens door to house with interior clearly out of 1971.

MIKEY
This house.  This house!  What a layout, just as I remembered it.  People are going to love it.

MIKA
Or, or.  They might just walk through this door and think first 'This is a lot of work.'

Mikey disregards comment.  

Sets coffee down and produces a camera. 

He chats with Nona he then takes one pic.  

He next heads to the kitchen and takes one pic, still chatting with Nona.

Mika proceeds to walk through the mostly empty house.

Peeks into outdated bathrooms and heavily wood paneled rumpus room with a massive, 1970s stereo player.

MIKEY
And whose bedroom was this?

NONA
Stella's.  Stella.  She had this wall covered with posters of bands, it was quite beautiful how she laid it out.  She was always an artist.  That's why she is Austin now.

MIKEY
Oh Austin, that's a place we might want to move to!  The market there is so hot.

Mikey again takes one picture of the bedroom room and moves on.

Mika flustered announces to no one.

MIKA
One picture.

MIKEY
Hmmm?

MIKA
One pic.  Each room you take one picture.  Why not take more than that?  You know, a selection to choose from.  Different angles.

MIKEY
Again,  I am a real estate agent.  I know this stuff.  One and done!

10 MINUTES LATER

INT. KITCHEN

Nona is at the kitchen table.

NONA
They are all signed.  All papers are signed.  You are our agent Mikey!

Mika is resigned. 

He also sits at the kitchen table.

MIKA
OK.  Great.  Great!

MIKEY
Hey, where is the pool table?  Is it still here?  In the basement.

NONA
Yes, it's still there!  We couldn't sell it during the estate sale.  It goes with the house.

MIKEY
Oh, bonus.  Hey, I'm going to play a game.  I need a pic of the basement, too! 

Mikey heads to the basement with Nona.  

NONA
Look, these pictures I painted for Mothers Day when I was in the fourth grade.  I need to take these before we sell the house!

Mika is now alone in the kitchen.

Mika says to no one. 

MIKA
Why not take more pictures.

INT. KITCHEN 

In Mika and Nona's house.  

MIKA sits At the kitchen table.  

He has  a laptop.  

NONA constructs some type of complicated dessert.

MIKA
Still no photos.  I got to call him.  I've got to. Got to.

Nona measures and then mixes.

NONA
Emmmm ... OK, OK.  We will call him.  You are so worried, it's annoying.  He will sell the house.

MIKA
I know.  I know.  He will sell ... just not sure when.  What is his number.

NONA
Just look on my phone!  There, by the mixer.

Mika finds a phone the by mixer.

He scrolls until he finds it.

Mika looks to see a Cyrillic recipe on the counter.

Mika then punches in a number on his cell phone.  

Mikey's voice is heard.

MIKEY
This is Mikey Derogai - the guy to sell your house!  How may I help you.

MIKA
Mikey

MIKEY
Yes.

MIKA
Mikey.

MIKEY
Yes this is he, who is this?

MIKA
Mika.  Nona.  It's us, the house you are selling.

MIKEY
Oh, Mika, Nona!  How are you!  What number is this, I've not seen it before.

MIKA
Is my cell phone.

MIKEY
You are in the contacts buddy! Any calls on the house yet, anything?

MIKA
Mikey, why would people be calling us?  You are the real estate agent,.

MIKEY
Well sometimes if they know the person selling, they call them and then you have to - 

MIKA
Look, no one has called.  And really - why would they - there is not single picture of the house.

MIKEY
What?

MIKA
On the site, the site, there's no pictures. None.

MIKEY
What?  What?

MIKA
The HOUSE.  The Realtors Plus site.  There is not one frigging picture of the house.  Not one.  Three days Mikey.

Nona looks condescendingly.  

He waves her off. 

She is back to measuring for a complicated dessert recipe.

NONA
Liters to ounces, what the fuck.  Why don't I know this!

MIKEY
Ohhhhh .... ohhhhh ... lemme check.

MIKA
See Mikey, you don't have to check.  I do that for you.  There are no pictures.

MIKEY
Ohhhh ... yeah ... this part.  Ummm ... give it 10 minutes and check.  The pics look great.  Hey, is Nona there?

NONA
Hi Mikey!  How many liters to the ounce.

MIKEY
Let me check my phone - 

Mika now sits at the table.

Mika is reading a book on small engine repair.

Nona is crouching and looking into an oven.

NONA
It's in.  60 minutes.

MIKA
At least there is no conversion on that, eh?

NONA
There is, centigrade to Fahrenheit.  It's an easy one though.  

Mikey pauses from reading. 

Looks at screen of laptop.

MIKA
OK, OK.  They are there.  And they ...

Pauses again.

MIKA
God dam it.  God dam it!

NONA
What?  What now!

MIKA
Come.  Come. Come look.  Witness it.

She turns from the coffee maker.

She was making a pot.  

Mika is smiling.

MIKA
Blurry. All are blurry.

NONA
What?

Nona comes closer to look.

MIKA
You don't have your frigging readers.  Trust me, every picture is blurry.  Every one.  Every.  How did he did he do it.  Not one, not two.  All!  How.

Nona is back to the coffee.

NONA
Something is wrong with the computer.

MIKA
Oh yeah, uh-huh. Something is wrong the computer.  Yeah.  OK.

NONA
OK, something is wrong his camera then.  We need to tell Mikey.  Honestly ...

MIKA
Oh yes, his camera is broken.  Sure.  Is broke.  Uh-huh.  Calling him - 

Mika is back to dialing the number.

MIKEY
This is Mikey Derogai.  The guy to sell your house.  How can I help you!

MIKA
By not having blurry pictures.

MIKEY
What, who is this?

MIKA
Mika, Nona.  Hello! Pictures are blurry!

MIKEY 
What pictures?

MIKA
Of the house!  The house.  All of the picture, except amazingly of the basement, are blurry. So if someone is interested in a pool table, dry basement, and rafters they are going to be very happy.  Probably lay out a bid on the spot.

Nona reads a magazine at the kitchen counter. 

She shouts.

NONA
Mikey, something must be wrong with your camera!

MIKEY
Oh hi Nona!  Yeah, OK, let me look at this.

MIKA
Why look, you said they looked great. Have someone else look.  You said the pictures looked great.  

MIKEY
Um ... um .... uh give me five minutes.

Both Nona and Mika sit at kitchen table in silence drinking coffee.  

Nona happily pages through magazine.

NONA
They are doing a production on women's voting rights.  That seems interesting!

MIKA
Yeah, what -

Nona's phone rights.  

She answers and places into speaker mode.

MIKEY
Nona it's Mikey, the pictures are not good.

MIKA
That we know.  That we are aware of.

MIKEY
I don't know why.  It's that camera.  That camera.  They looked amazing in screen on the camera -

MIKA
Who cares.  When can we take more.  Is nice day, we can do it today or tomorrow.  

MIKEY
Well, it will have to be Monday.

MIKA
Its frigging Thursday Mikey!  Thursday.

MIKEY
Well, I am not in Aurora.

MIKA
Where the hell are you, Saigon?

MIKEY
No, Cleveland.

MIKA
Cleveland?

NONA
Cleveland!  Oh, we have many relatives there Mikey, I can tell you where to go!

MIKEY
Oh, thanks Nona! 

